\description{
Computes the net benefit of using a model (treat if predicted risk is at
or above the threshold), of treating everyone, and of treating no one, at
each risk threshold, for a binary, survival or competing-risks outcome,
for one model or several models validated on the same patients.
This is the core quantity every other voipred validation metric (ΔNB,
P(useful), P(best), EVPI, EVSI) is built on.
}
\details{
\strong{Binary outcome.} With outcome \eqn{Y}, predicted risk \eqn{\hat\pi} and
threshold \eqn{z},
\deqn{NB_{model}(z) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_i Y_i T_i - \frac{1}{n}\sum_i (1-Y_i) T_i \frac{z}{1-z},\quad T_i = 1(\hat\pi_i \ge z)}
\deqn{NB_{all}(z) = \bar Y - (1-\bar Y)\frac{z}{1-z}, \qquad NB_{none}(z) = 0.}

\strong{Survival outcome} (\code{Surv(time, status)}, with a \code{horizon}). The event is
"the event by the horizon". With \eqn{q} the share treated at threshold
\eqn{z} and \eqn{F_+} the risk of the event by the horizon among them,
\eqn{NB_{model} = q F_+ - q (1 - F_+) z/(1-z)}. Two estimators:
\itemize{
\item Kaplan-Meier (default, \code{cipcw} not given): \eqn{F_+} and the overall risk are
Kaplan-Meier estimates (Aalen-Johansen for competing risks), as in
dcurves. If none of the treated patients is followed up to the horizon,
the net benefit at that threshold is \code{NA}, with a warning.
\item cIPCW, when \code{cipcw} is given: conditional inverse probability of censoring
weighting, which allows censoring to depend on covariates. Patients with
the event by the horizon are weighted by \eqn{1/\hat G(T_i- \mid X_i)},
patients event-free at the horizon by \eqn{1/\hat G(\tau \mid X_i)}, and
patients censored before the horizon get no weight. The risk by the
horizon is \eqn{\sum_{events} w_i / n}; sensitivity and specificity are
weighted proportions, and \eqn{NB = \pi Se - (1-\pi)(1-Sp) z/(1-z)}.
Usually \code{cipcw} is just the covariates of the censoring, e.g.
\code{cipcw = ~ age + nodes}: voipred fits a Cox model of the censoring times
of \code{data} on them and truncates the probabilities of remaining uncensored
at 0.02 (weights at 50). See \code{\link[=cipcw_control]{cipcw_control()}} for your own censoring
model, given weights, or other truncation.
}

\strong{Competing risks} (\code{Surv(time, status)} with a factor status whose first
level is censoring). The event is \code{cause} (default: the first event level)
by the horizon; a patient with a competing event by the horizon counts as
not having the event. The model must predict the cumulative incidence of
that cause; for competing risks only true censoring is censoring.

A patient is treated when the predicted risk is at or above the threshold,
and a predicted risk of exactly 0 is never treated, as in dcurves.

\strong{Prior.} \code{nb_val()} works in a Bayesian framework, which the
value-of-information functions (\code{\link[=p_useful]{p_useful()}}, \code{\link[=p_best]{p_best()}}, \code{\link[=evpi]{evpi()}},
\code{\link[=evsi]{evsi()}}) use to represent the uncertainty of the net benefit. By default
it uses no prior information: with the improper prior \code{\link[=nb_improper]{nb_improper()}}
(Beta(0, 0)) only the data are used, and the net benefit is exactly the
usual one, \eqn{TP/n - FP/n \cdot z/(1-z)}. For a binary outcome, prior
information can be added with another prior (\code{\link[=nb_uniform]{nb_uniform()}},
\code{\link[=nb_jeffreys]{nb_jeffreys()}}, or your own with \code{\link[=nb_beta]{nb_beta()}}; see \link{nb_priors}): the
prevalence \eqn{\pi} and, at each threshold, the sensitivity \eqn{Se}
and specificity \eqn{Sp} of the model then have Beta priors, updated with
the validation data (as in bayesDCA), and the net benefit columns are the
posterior means, \eqn{E[\pi] E[Se] - (1 - E[\pi])(1 - E[Sp]) z/(1-z)} and
\eqn{E[\pi] - (1 - E[\pi]) z/(1-z)}. Survival and competing-risks
outcomes use only the improper prior: their net benefit is the usual
Kaplan-Meier, Aalen-Johansen or cIPCW estimate.

\strong{Predicted risks.} Validation needs only the predicted risks of the
patients in \code{data}: give them with \code{predicted_risk}, a numeric vector with
one risk per row of \code{data}, e.g. \code{predicted_risk = val$p}. The name of the
column of \code{data} that holds them works too: \code{predicted_risk = "p"} is the
same as \code{predicted_risk = val$p} when \code{data = val}. The model
object is not needed, and the risks can come from anywhere (an R model,
published coefficients, other software). The outcome alone decides the type
of analysis (binary, survival or competing risks).

\strong{Several models.} To compare models, give the risks of each, all
predicted for the same patients (the rows of \code{data}), in one call; every
other argument (outcome, thresholds, horizon, cause, \code{cipcw}, prior,
weights) applies to all of them:
\itemize{
\item a named list, \code{predicted_risk = list(Full = val$p1, Age = val$p2)};
\item the names of columns of \code{data}, \code{predicted_risk = c("p1", "p2")}, named to
relabel them, \code{c(Full = "p1", Age = "p2")};
\item a data frame or matrix with one column per model,
\code{predicted_risk = val[c("p1", "p2")]};
\item fitted models, \code{model = list(Full = fit1, Age = fit2)}, or a list that
mixes risks and models, \code{predicted_risk = list(Full = fit1, Age = val$p2)}.
}

Each model is labelled by its name in the list (or column), otherwise by
the expression in the call (\code{list(val$p1, fit2)} gives \code{p1} and \code{fit2}),
or \code{"Model 1"}, \code{"Model 2"}, ...; \code{label} relabels them. The result is the
same as computing each model separately and combining the results with
\link[=c.voipred_nb]{c()}, and the value-of-information functions draw the
models jointly, so their correlation on the same patients is kept. With
cIPCW, one censoring model (and one set of censoring weights) is used for
all the models.

\strong{Predicting the risks from a model (optional).} Instead of the risks,
the fitted model can be given, as \code{model} (or in place of \code{predicted_risk},
e.g. \code{nb_val(val, fit, "y")}). voipred then predicts the risks of the patients
in \code{data} from it, once, and every later step (the value-of-information
functions, plots) reuses them: the probability of the event (binary), of
the event by \code{horizon} (survival), or of \code{cause} by \code{horizon} (competing
risks). There are two ways:
\itemize{
\item \strong{By default}, from the model alone. voipred keeps no list of models: it
uses \code{predict()} with \code{type = "response"} or \code{type = "prob"}, or
\code{riskRegression::predictRisk()} (at \code{horizon}, and for \code{cause}), and its
own prediction code only for models these get wrong. Checked in the
tests against each package's own predictions:
\itemize{
\item binary: \code{glm} (logit, probit, cloglog), \code{logistf}, \code{mgcv::gam},
\code{earth}, \code{rpart}, \code{partykit::ctree}, \code{randomForest}, \code{ranger}
(\code{probability = TRUE}), \code{gbm}, \code{xgboost}, \code{lightgbm}, \code{catboost},
\code{glmnet}, \code{cv.glmnet}, \code{e1071::svm} (\code{probability = TRUE}),
\code{e1071::naiveBayes}, \code{naivebayes}, \code{nnet}, \code{MASS::lda}, \code{MASS::qda},
\code{kknn}, and bayespred \code{bpmfit} and \code{bpm} (posterior mean of risk);
\item survival: \code{coxph}, \code{rms::cph}, \code{survreg}, \code{rms::psm}, \code{flexsurvreg},
\code{flexsurvspline}, \code{rstpm2::stpm2}, \code{mgcv::gam} with \code{cox.ph()},
\code{ranger}, \code{randomForestSRC}, \code{aorsf}, \code{partykit::ctree}, \code{prodlim}, and
bayespred.dev Bayesian Cox and parametric survival models (posterior
mean of risk);
\item competing risks: \code{riskRegression::CSC}, \code{riskRegression::FGR} (fitted
for the requested cause), \code{cmprsk::crr}, competing-risks
\code{randomForestSRC}, and survival's multi-state \code{coxph}.
}

Any other model whose \code{predict()} returns probabilities in the usual way,
or that has a \code{riskRegression::predictRisk()} method, works too. Models
that only give relative risks or times (e.g. \code{gbm} and \code{glmnet} Cox
models, \code{rpart} survival trees) have no route to probabilities. Before
predicting, the covariates the model uses are compared with the columns
of \code{data}, and missing ones are named in an error.
\item \strong{With \code{predict_function}}, a function you write that returns the risks.
It works for any model, and is the way to go when voipred stops with
"Could not get predicted risks from this ... model". If given, it is
always used.
}

Whichever way they are given, the risks are checked: one per row, no \code{NA},
values in [0, 1] (log-odds, relative risks or predicted times are
rejected as scores), and not only 0s and 1s (rejected as class labels).
With a model, where the model's own outcome can be read from its formula,
voipred also stops or
warns if it does not match \code{outcome} (e.g. a survival model with a binary
outcome, or a binary model with a survival outcome), and warns if
\code{horizon} is beyond the model's own follow-up.

What voipred cannot check is the meaning of valid probabilities. With
\code{predict_function}, make sure it returns the risk of the event (not the
survival probability), by \code{horizon} and for \code{cause}.
}
\examples{
df_dev_binary <- gusto[1:5000, ]
df_val_binary <- gusto[5001:10000, ]
fit <- glm(day30 ~ age + Killip + sysbp + pulse,
           family = binomial, data = df_dev_binary)

# Binary outcome: the predicted risks are all that is needed
df_val_binary$p <- predict(fit, newdata = df_val_binary, type = "response")
res <- nb_val(data = df_val_binary,
              predicted_risk = df_val_binary$p,
              outcome = "day30",
              thresholds = seq(0, 0.3, by = 0.01))
res
summary(res)
plot(res)

# The same, with the name of the column of `data` that holds the risks
nb_val(data = df_val_binary,
       predicted_risk = "p",
       outcome = "day30",
       thresholds = c(0.05, 0.1))

# Several models: their risks in one call
fit_age <- glm(day30 ~ age, family = binomial, data = df_dev_binary)
df_val_binary$p_age <- predict(fit_age, newdata = df_val_binary, type = "response")
both <- nb_val(data = df_val_binary,
               predicted_risk = list(Full = df_val_binary$p, `Age only` = df_val_binary$p_age),
               outcome = "day30",
               thresholds = seq(0, 0.3, by = 0.01))
both
summary(both, thresholds = c(0.05, 0.1))
plot(both)
# the same with column names, or with the models
nb_val(data = df_val_binary,
       predicted_risk = c(Full = "p", `Age only` = "p_age"),
       outcome = "day30",
       thresholds = c(0.05, 0.1))
nb_val(data = df_val_binary,
       model = list(Full = fit, `Age only` = fit_age),
       outcome = "day30",
       thresholds = c(0.05, 0.1))

# Optionally, the model instead of the risks: by default, or with your own
# prediction function
nb_val(data = df_val_binary,
       model = fit,
       outcome = "day30",
       thresholds = c(0.05, 0.1))
nb_val(data = df_val_binary,
       model = fit,
       outcome = "day30",
       thresholds = c(0.05, 0.1),
       predict_function = function(model, newdata, ...)
     predict(model, newdata, type = "response"))

# Optional prior information (binary outcome); the default uses the data only
nb_val(data = df_val_binary,
       predicted_risk = df_val_binary$p,
       outcome = "day30",
       thresholds = c(0.05, 0.1),
       prior = nb_uniform())
nb_val(data = df_val_binary,
       predicted_risk = df_val_binary$p,
       outcome = "day30",
       thresholds = c(0.05, 0.1),
       prior = nb_beta(prevalence = c(7, 93)))

# Survival: death by 5 years (Rotterdam breast cancer data)
library(survival)
rott <- rotterdam
rott$years <- rott$dtime / 365.25
df_dev_survival <- rott[1:1500, ]
df_val_survival <- rott[1501:2982, ]
cox <- coxph(Surv(years, death) ~ age + size + nodes + grade,
             data = df_dev_survival)
# the predicted risks of death by 5 years
sf <- survfit(cox, newdata = df_val_survival)
df_val_survival$p5 <- 1 - summary(sf, times = 5)$surv[1, ]
nb_val(data = df_val_survival,
       predicted_risk = df_val_survival$p5,
       outcome = ~ Surv(years, death),
       horizon = 5,
       thresholds = c(0.1, 0.2, 0.3))

# Optionally, the model instead of the risks: by default, or with your own
# prediction function (here the same risks as above)
nb_val(data = df_val_survival,
       model = cox,
       outcome = ~ Surv(years, death),
       horizon = 5,
       thresholds = c(0.1, 0.2, 0.3))
nb_val(data = df_val_survival,
       model = cox,
       outcome = ~ Surv(years, death),
       horizon = 5,
       thresholds = c(0.1, 0.2, 0.3),
       predict_function = function(model, newdata, horizon, ...)
     1 - summary(survfit(model, newdata = newdata), times = horizon)$surv[1, ])

# The same with conditional IPCW: censoring depends on age, nodes and grade
# (a Cox model of the censoring times, fitted by voipred)
nb_val(data = df_val_survival,
       predicted_risk = df_val_survival$p5,
       outcome = ~ Surv(years, death),
       horizon = 5,
       thresholds = c(0.1, 0.2, 0.3),
       cipcw = ~ age + nodes + grade)

# or your own censoring model, fitted to the validation data
cens <- coxph(Surv(years, death == 0) ~ age + nodes + grade,
              data = df_val_survival)
nb_val(data = df_val_survival,
       predicted_risk = df_val_survival$p5,
       outcome = ~ Surv(years, death),
       horizon = 5,
       thresholds = c(0.1, 0.2, 0.3),
       cipcw = cens)
}
